Solve the inequality
\[\dfrac{x+1}{x+2}>\dfrac{3x+4}{2x+9}.\]

Put your final answer within \boxed{}.

\textbf{Solution:}

\begin{align*}
\dfrac{x+1}{x+2} &> \dfrac{3x+4}{2x+9} \\
\dfrac{2x+9}{2x+9} &> \dfrac{3x+4}{2x+9} \\
2x+9 &> 3x+4 \\
5 &> x \\
x &< 5 \\
\boxed{(-\infty,5)} &< x \\
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:}

\boxed{(-\infty,5)}

\textbf{Explanation:}

To solve the inequality, we first need to find a common denominator for the two fractions. The common denominator is $(2x+9)(x+2)$. We then multiply both sides of the inequality by this common denominator to eliminate the fractions. This gives us the inequality $2x+9 > 3x+4$. We then solve this inequality for $x$ to get $x < 5$. Finally, we express the solution in interval notation to get $(-\infty,5)$.

